)−E(t0))/h(k). Discharge L2 and quantify the arbitrary sequence h: every sequence of nonzero increments in this punctured η-window tending to zero gives energy quotients tending to ∫ e_t(x,t0) dx.","uses":["M001","N002"],"uses_local":["F003"],"objects":["V","w","h","e","E","t0","η","n","dx"],"produces":[{"id":"F004","statement":"For every sequence zeta:ℕ→ℝ, if 0<|zeta(k)|<η for every k and zeta(k)→0, then (E(t0+zeta(k))−E(t0))/zeta(k)→∫_{ℝ^n}e_t(x,t0) dx.","objects":["E","e","t0","η","n","dx"],"assumptions":["L1"],"binders":[{"symbol":"zeta","type":"real sequence"},{"symbol":"k","type":"natural sequence index"},{"symbol":"x","type":"integration variable in ℝ^n"}]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":["L2"]},{"id":"S005","text":"F004 implies the full punctured limit as the real increment tends to zero. Indeed failure of that limit would give some fixed positive error and arbitrarily small nonzero increments within the η-window whose quotient errors are at least that amount. Choosing one at distance less than 1/(k+1) for each natural k would form a sequence contradicting F004. This is the sequential criterion for limits in ℝ, with the entire window inside positive time. Thus the actual finite integral E is differentiable at t0 and
