Combine this with F001 and the integrability exported in N002. Discharge L1 and universally quantify t0; no choices C,R,η,B enter the conclusion. This proves the claimed derivative formula and integrability.","uses":["N002"],"uses_local":["F004","F001"],"objects":["E","e","t0","η","u","n","dx"],"produces":[],"introduces":[],"discharges":["L1"]}],"applications":[{"input":"M004","at":"S002","bindings":"For each x∈Q and nonzero h0 with |h0|<η, take f(r)=e(x,r) and f'(r)=e_t(x,r) on positive r, extend both by zero elsewhere, and take a=min(t0,t0+h0), b=max(t0,t0+h0).","hypothesis_checks":["h0≠0 gives a<b.","H002 and N002 give smoothness and hence continuity on this closed interval, contained in the positive time window.","The time derivative on that interval is e_t by H002 and F001; no extension point at r≤0 lies in the interval."],"id":"A001"},{"input":"M001","at":"S004","bindings":"Set A=ℝ^n with its Lebesgue measurable space and μ=dx, G=ℝ, source F(k)=V(k), source f(x)=e_t(x,t0), and source bound=w=B 1_Q.","hypothesis_checks":["The Euclidean Lebesgue space is a measurable space with measure dx; ℝ is a normed additive commutative group and real normed space.","F003 gives almost everywhere strong measurability of each V(k), from its continuity.","F003 and F002 give integrability of w, since it is B times the indicator of the finite-measure box Q.","F003 gives the pointwise bound |V(k)(x)|≤w(x), which implies the required almost everywhere bounds.","F003 gives convergence to e_t(x,t0) at every x from time differentiability, hence almost everywhere."],"id":"A002"}]},{"id":"R004","inputs":["H001","H002","N002","M002","M003"],"output":"N004","definitions":["D001","D002","D003"],"rules":["direct","forall_introduction","implication_introduction","exists_elimination"],"locals":[{"symbol":"t0","type":"real time","scope":"R004/argument","start":"S001","end":"S003","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix real t0 and assume L1: t0>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},