N002 says the derivative and each summand are continuous, supported in C, and integrable on ℝ^n and hence on Q.","uses":["H001","H002","N002"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["t0","j","C","R","Q","g","u","n"],"produces":[{"id":"F001","statement":"g is smooth and zero near the boundary of Q. Its derivative ∂_{x_j}g=∂_{x_j}u_t(·,t0)·∂_{x_j}u(·,t0)+u_t(·,t0)·∂_{x_jx_j}u(·,t0); the derivative and both summands are supported in C and absolutely integrable.","objects":["g","u","j","t0","C","Q"],"assumptions":["L1"],"binders":[]}],"introduces":["L1"],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S002","text":"Let iota(y,r) insert the real r in coordinate j, with y∈ℝ^(n−1) giving all remaining coordinates in their original order, and put phi(y)(r)=g(iota(y,r)). For each y, phi(y) is smooth on ℝ with derivative r↦∂_{x_j}g(iota(y,r)). Its endpoint values at −R and R are zero, since those points have a coordinate on the boundary of Q and in any event lie outside C. Apply M003 with real codomain, endpoints −R,R, function phi(y), and the displayed derivative. The derivative exists at every point of [−R,R] and is continuous, hence interval integrable there. We obtain
