This holds for every y∈ℝ^(n−1). The oriented integral agrees with the Lebesgue integral on the closed interval here, since −R<R and the endpoints have measure zero.","uses":["M003"],"uses_local":["F001"],"objects":["iota","phi","g","j","n","R","Q","C","dx"],"produces":[{"id":"F002","statement":"For every y∈ℝ^(n−1), ∫_{[−R,R]}∂_{x_j}g(iota(y,r)) dr=0.","objects":["g","j","iota","R","n"],"assumptions":["L1"],"binders":[{"symbol":"y","type":"remaining-coordinate vector in ℝ^(n−1)"},{"symbol":"r","type":"real integration variable"}]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S003","text":"Set Qrest=[−R,R]^(n−1). Under iota, Qrest×[−R,R] is exactly Q with its coordinates reordered. Standard Lebesgue measure is the product of the coordinate measures (D001); reordering these factors preserves the measure, since on any coordinate rectangle both descriptions give the product of the same side lengths. Thus the integral on Q is the product-measure integral of (y,r)↦∂_{x_j}g(iota(y,r)) with Lebesgue measures restricted to Qrest and [−R,R]. This scalar function is continuous on a compact box and bounded there, hence integrable for that finite product measure. M002 now gives
